{
  "concept": "A batted ball's flight is a parabola in the distance–height plane, so whether it clears an outfield wall is simply a comparison of the curve's height with the wall's height at one distance.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal axis labeled 'Distance (ft)' from 0 to 600 with ticks and labels every 100 ft, arrowhead at right end",
    "Vertical axis labeled 'Height (ft)' from 0 to about 320 with labeled ticks at 100, 200, 300, arrowhead at top",
    "Faint dotted gridlines at every 100 ft horizontally and every 50 ft vertically",
    "Teal parabolic trajectory starting at the origin, peaking slightly above 150 ft near 290 ft, landing just past 560 ft",
    "Small teal arrowheads spaced along the curve indicating direction of travel",
    "Open circular marker on the rising branch near 200 ft (draggable position marker)",
    "Leader line and label 'Trajectory of ball' pointing to the marker",
    "Leader line and label 'Position of hitter' pointing to the origin",
    "Short red rectangle standing on the x-axis at 400 ft representing the outfield wall",
    "Label 'Outfield wall' above the red rectangle",
    "Dashed vertical drop line from the moving marker down to the x-axis",
    "Dynamic readout box showing current distance and ball height",
    "Comparison annotation showing ball height vs wall height at the wall's distance ('CLEARS' / 'HITS WALL')",
    "Sampled-points view: discrete teal dots at evenly spaced distances with an accompanying small value table of (distance, height) pairs"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "ballDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Distance from hitter (ft)",
      "range": [0, 570, 5],
      "default": 200,
      "teaches": "Height is a function of horizontal distance: every x along the flight has exactly one height, rising then falling symmetrically about the peak."
    },
    {
      "id": "wallDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Outfield wall distance (ft)",
      "range": [100, 600, 10],
      "default": 400,
      "teaches": "Clearing the wall is a comparison of two heights at a single x-value; move the wall far enough out and the same hit becomes an out."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Smooth path", "Sampled points"],
      "default": "Smooth path",
      "teaches": "The continuous arc and a table of discrete distance–height samples describe the same motion; the curve is the limit of the sampled data."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The flight path",
      "narration": "The ball leaves the hitter at the origin and follows a downward-opening parabola, peaking a little above 150 ft near 290 ft before landing past 560 ft.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 200, "wallDistance": 400, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read a height at a distance",
      "narration": "Drag the marker along the curve: the dashed drop line to the axis and the readout show the ball's height at that exact distance from home plate.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 290, "wallDistance": 400, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Does it clear the wall?",
      "narration": "At the 400 ft wall the ball is still well above 100 ft — far over the barrier's top, so this is a home run.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 400, "wallDistance": 400, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push the wall back",
      "narration": "Move the wall out toward 550 ft and the descending branch drops below its top: the identical hit now fails to clear.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 550, "wallDistance": 550, "viewMode": "Smooth path" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Curve as data",
      "narration": "Switch to sampled points: the same arc appears as a set of (distance, height) pairs, linking the graph to a table of values.",
      "state": { "ballDistance": 290, "wallDistance": 400, "viewMode": "Sampled points" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Clearing the outfield wall is not about how far the ball travels — it is about the ball's height at the wall's distance, one vertical comparison on the parabola.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape plot on white with black axes drawn as arrows. X-axis 'Distance (ft)' 0–600, labeled every 100; Y-axis 'Height (ft)' 0–320, labeled at 100/200/300. Light gray dotted gridlines. The trajectory is a thick teal (#1a7f9c) parabola from (0,0) through a vertex near (290,155) to about (565,0), with 4–6 small teal arrowheads along it pointing in the direction of flight. An open teal circle marks the current distance, with a thin gray dashed line dropping to the x-axis and a small readout of distance and height nearby. A short thick red rectangle sits on the axis at the wall distance (about 10 ft wide, ~15 ft tall in data units) with the label 'Outfield wall' above it in black. Black leader arrows carry the labels 'Position of hitter' (to the origin) and 'Trajectory of ball' (to the marker). In sampled-points mode the smooth curve is replaced by ~15 evenly spaced teal filled dots at the same heights, with a compact two-column list of distance/height values in a corner. Highlight the comparison at the wall with a green 'CLEARS' or orange 'HITS WALL' tag."
}

